#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/id #uuid "a3f9e797-4bfb-5b2a-b460-98d7def3ae20" :question/math-academy-id "e-6754" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/id #uuid "83fc553b-3ad0-5003-afe3-81204a695162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6755" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "a2641aac-7c3a-5a2c-97f0-aca4782c7215" :question/math-academy-id "e-7220" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} {:answer/id #uuid "76e51c07-ae30-5f76-9211-c622eb4edaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251534} {:answer/id #uuid "7db2cbba-40a7-517d-85d4-4db5c32721c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251608} {:answer/id #uuid "c42fdbb0-808e-537e-ae57-9e0a4f2d9b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252292} {:answer/id #uuid "e30522a4-eaa9-513c-932a-1eb516b48504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e46c9e-0f1c-54e1-8afd-4cf347c19516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a5d4f00-fa86-576c-9cad-eafafdb18fe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117411" :question/problem "$-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a16dec-d97e-5d58-8140-4f946ba70b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058114} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd3e641-157c-5731-811c-9fafad5d4e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058115} {:answer/id #uuid "c198fabc-f37e-57f0-9183-91fbfc924cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058116} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8a4b8a-43da-54c1-a4bc-d7424d096a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058117} {:answer/id #uuid "0916b252-4eec-5145-a7ee-432d6ab43717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058118}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0114138c-2ef8-582d-af73-d2fbea6e795c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058113}] :question/id #uuid "a899c299-0869-5669-9b1c-ab47bfeb6793" :question/math-academy-id "q-24760" :question/problem "What is $\\arcsin (-1)$?"}]}
